WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.670
Film Courage: …And we’re rolling, we have
sound?

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:02.670
Okay, great.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.160
And we’ll hand the bag to you.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:11.050
Justin Warren, filmmaker: [Grabs bag with
questions in it]…Ooops, sorry.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.340
Film Courage: Okay, this is from Smeltzer
Sounds…hello.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:21.779
“The itch for a project or idea, how it
feels, why you can’t stop thinking about

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:26.630
it, it’s a project that has to get done…what
was that first project like for you?”

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.910
Justin: What was the first project like that?

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:33.530
Man…there’s a lot of projects like that
actually.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:41.410
Like I kind of have a hard drive of scripts
that I just desperately want to make.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:48.059
But the thing that I try to do was find the
great intersection between that feeling he

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:49.530
was talking about.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:54.550
That I desperately have to make this, I want
to have this exist and I’m ready to lay

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.100
down on the train tracks to make this work.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:06.540
That feeling intersecting with kind of like
realistic resources of where I am right now.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:14.939
And so I have script that take place in fantasy
worlds where it would take phenomenal amounts

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:21.430
of money to build just the world and those
are scripts I desperately want to make but

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.889
I’m just not at that level right now.

00:01:23.889 --> 00:01:29.499
So the kind of thing for me is that it kind
of has to tick two boxes.

00:01:29.499 --> 00:01:34.990
It has to tick the feeling of “I just have
to make this.”

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.439
And “Is this realistic for me to make.”

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:43.889
Because I think one of the hardest things
I learned was one of the first movie because

00:01:43.889 --> 00:01:50.819
THEN THERE WAS JOE (which was my first feature)
was actually the second script that I landed

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:53.590
on to want to make after school.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.049
So I’d written an entire other feature that
I was like “I’m going to make this one.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:59.099
I’m totally going to take this one.”

00:01:59.099 --> 00:02:06.770
And it was funny, I was talking to a producer
friend of mine (not the producer of the movie,

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:10.750
but a producer friend) and I was saying like
“I really want to make this movie.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.340
What do you think I should do?”

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.220
And they were like “First off, you should
write a business plan.”

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:22.150
And I was like “Ugh, no!”

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:26.319
So you can know realistically if you can make
this or not.

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:28.360
And I was just like “Ah, man.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.709
That sounds pretty awful, but I’ll do it
anyway.”

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:36.600
So I sat down and for like a couple weeks
I budgeted out this script and I figured out

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.860
very quickly this is like a million dollar
movie.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:45.409
And I thought just in terms of (perhaps cyclically,
I don’t know if realistic is the way to

00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:52.140
put it) but cynically I thought who would
give me a million dollars to make a movie?

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.400
Like who would do that?

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.819
And I don’t think anybody would do that?

00:02:56.819 --> 00:03:02.500
And so I was like “Okay, I need to try and
figure out something else.”

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:08.739
A way that I could gather some amount of money
(every movie costs someone amount of money).

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:13.330
It’s not possible to make something with
nothing, you have to have some amount of money.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:17.420
And so I was like “Okay well I’m going
to do what I can to write something that has

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.049
that feeling of like I have to make this.”

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:28.090
Along with something that where it is cost-effective.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.829
So then I wrote THEN THERE WAS JOE thinking
like “Okay, I’ going to shoot this back

00:03:31.829 --> 00:03:32.829
home where I’m from.

00:03:32.829 --> 00:03:36.860
I’m going to shoot it in my parent’s house
where I grew up.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:42.010
I know for a fact there’s a guy down the
street who owns this place who probably will

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:47.420
let us shoot there for free” and so that’s
what I was thinking in terms of how to get

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.640
it made.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:53.879
And so if I tried to do that with the other
film, I think I’d still be trying to make

00:03:53.879 --> 00:03:54.879
it.

00:03:54.879 --> 00:04:00.450
I’d still be trying to come up with the
money because to be frank I’m still a first-time

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:06.629
filmmaker (I just completed my first feature),
I’m still like “Who would give me money

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:07.980
for my next one?

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.349
I don’t even know?”

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:14.999
And so I’m still sort of in that mindset
of like “Well, okay I know for a fact I

00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:17.739
want to make something else.

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:24.949
It has to resonate with me and right now where
I am, it has to be made in a way that is realistic,

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:28.919
where I’m still going to be calling in favors
most likely.

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:34.920
I think for my next film I think I can get
a larger sum of money but not too much, you

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:38.500
know?

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.930
My goal eventually is to like stair-step my
way into it like each film just gets a little

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:51.240
bit bigger than the last one which is not
too unreasonable.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:58.770
I think it’s easy to think “Oh man, I
made this movie and now my next movie is going

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.800
to be a 30 million dollar movie.”

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.050
And maybe I’m cynical but I just think that’s
unrealistic.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.729
Maybe that happens for other people.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:08.860
It does happen for other people but I don’t
know if it would happen for me.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:16.800
But my goal is just to make films that I feel
like I have to make that are within a certain

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:23.810
contained, that can be made in a relatively
contained way.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:30.250
And what’s great about that right now is
when I was thinking about that first script

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:36.160
that I wanted to make that was like a million
dollars now in hindsight I would never trust

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.449
Justin two years ago with a million dollars,
I would never do that.

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:43.740
Because I just didn’t have the chops then.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.819
I feel like now I could probably do that,
maybe?

00:05:47.819 --> 00:05:50.610
I think?

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:59.210
But it’s just one of those things like I
feel filmmaking especially directing and writing

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:04.699
it’s a process to get up (to direct) a huge
movie.

00:06:04.699 --> 00:06:10.349
And I’m just going to slowly, kind of march
my way up the stairs until hopefully I’ll

00:06:10.349 --> 00:06:13.050
get to direct something really big one day.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:19.319
Until then I’m going to try and make films
within my means that scratch both of those

00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:20.319
boxes.

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:25.550
Film Courage: I think it’s great that you
wouldn’t be too tempted…I mean who wouldn’t

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:29.180
want 2 million to make something but I mean
so many people have over-confidence sometimes

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:35.180
and I think it’s kind of this thing that’s
definitely embraced and I think that’s great

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:42.680
that you want to go to these next levels in
a progression not just totally jump.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:48.129
Justin: Yeah because I think if I were to
just jump to a huge movie right now, like

00:06:48.129 --> 00:06:50.889
I don’t know how good that movie would be?

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:55.150
Because there’s so many, you know there’s
that whole thing “Mo’ Money, Mo’ Problems.”

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.919
Like that’s kind of a real thing.

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:03.630
So my last movie was of a small budget range.

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:09.800
The next script that I wrote is about twice
that but twice of a little is still not very

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.580
much, you know what I mean?

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:12.760
And I think I can handle that.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:18.270
I think I can handle how to make something
really solid in a very fiscally responsible

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.650
way.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.850
And I like that.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:28.900
I’ve actually learned to appreciate kind
of slow growth and slow progression and being

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.220
cool with that.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:34.789
Because like I was talking about earlier,
it’s really easy to just want to get there

00:07:34.789 --> 00:07:35.789
right now.

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:38.500
“I want to direct the net AVENGERS movie
right now!”

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:45.440
You can really want that but if we’re realistic
about it I wouldn’t trust myself right now

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.580
at this particular spot in my career, I wouldn’t
trust myself with an AVENGERS movie.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.189
There’s no way!

00:07:53.189 --> 00:07:58.639
And so when I get there, when I get to the
point where I can direct something like that,

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:05.060
I want the experience and the resourcefulness
to all line up hopefully in a way where synergy

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:12.130
is occurring where I can actually meet that
opportunity and do a good job with it.

